---
export const prerender = true;

import RoomLayout from '../../../layouts/RoomLayout.astro';
import ZybachNav from '../../../components/zybach/ZybachNav.astro';
import ContentNote from '../../../components/zybach/ContentNote.astro';
import WorkingEditionBanner from '../../../components/zybach/WorkingEditionBanner.astro';
import { collectionStatement } from '../../../components/zybach/_data';

/* The collection statement, adopted for the working edition. */
const FALLBACK_STATEMENT =
  'The Soap Creek Valley oral histories were recorded between 1989 and 2009 and print the words of narrators born between 1890 and 1930, together with historical documents reproduced as appendices. Some interviews contain terms for African Americans, American Indians, and people of Asian, Mexican and European descent, and language about women and disability, that readers will find offensive today. Some contain personal information about named individuals — health, deaths, criminal allegations, addresses — and describe the locations of American Indian burial and artifact sites. The narrators reviewed and approved these transcripts. Nothing has been altered. Page-level notes identify specific passages so readers can approach the material knowingly.';

/* One substitution against the generated statement, and only one: the clause
   about burial and artifact sites has to say that those pages are held, not
   merely that they exist. Written as a replacement rather than a second copy
   of the statement so that the generated text stays the source. */
const SITE_CLAUSE_WAS = 'and describe the locations of American Indian burial and artifact sites.';
const SITE_CLAUSE_NOW =
  'and some describe the locations of American Indian burial and artifact sites; those pages are held while the Library consults the Confederated Tribes of Grand Ronde and the Confederated Tribes of Siletz Indians.';

const statement = (collectionStatement() ?? FALLBACK_STATEMENT).replace(
  SITE_CLAUSE_WAS,
  SITE_CLAUSE_NOW,
);
---

<RoomLayout
  room="collections"
  title="Content note"
  subtitle="What is in this collection that a reader should know about before reading it."
  width="prose"
  sourceId="collections-zybach-content-note"
  parents={[
    { label: 'The Zybach Collection', href: '/collections/zybach/' },
  ]}
>
  <span data-pagefind-meta="collection:zybach" hidden></span>

  <div class="source-page">
    <ZybachNav current="finding-aid" />
    <WorkingEditionBanner />

    <h2 id="statement">The collection statement</h2>

    <ContentNote text={statement} level="collection" draft={false} />

    <h2 id="practice">How the Library handles this</h2>

    <p>
      Archives describe; they do not edit. The transcripts in this collection
      were corrected and approved by the people who spoke them, and the Library
      does not alter what a narrator approved, soften a word, or quietly drop a
      passage. Instead it notes.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>At page level.</strong> Where a specific passage carries
        offensive language or sensitive personal detail, a short neutral note
        appears on that page saying so. The note describes; it does not quote.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>At collection level.</strong> The statement above appears on the
        finding aid, so nobody meets this material without warning.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Held pages.</strong> A page whose content is personal data about
        named individuals — a 1941 directory of addresses and telephone numbers,
        an appendix naming children, a table of landowners and tax lots — is
        held. The page still exists in the sequence and says it is held, and
        why. Nothing is silently missing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Withheld places.</strong> Pages describing the locations of
        American Indian burial, grave or artifact sites are held, and so are the
        index entries, place pages and timeline entries that would lead a reader
        to them. They stay held while the Library consults the Confederated
        Tribes of Grand Ronde and the Confederated Tribes of Siletz Indians.
        Whether to describe those places at all is not the Library's decision
        alone. Kalapuya history itself stays: what is withheld is where things
        are, not whose they were.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="osu">Oregon State's own notice</h2>

    <p>
      Oregon State University's Special Collections and Archives Research Center
      holds the recordings and publishes its own statement about this language,
      dated April 2025, on the
      <a href="https://scarc.library.oregonstate.edu/findingaids/?p=collections/findingaid&id=2309">finding
      aid for OH 006, the Soap Creek Valley History Project Oral Histories</a>.
      It reads, in part: "Please be aware that some of the Soap Creek Valley
      History Project Oral Histories include racist terms that may be disturbing
      or activating. Specifically, the interviewer, in attempting to collect
      demographic information about early-twentieth-century Corvallis, uses
      derogatory terms for African Americans and East Asians. The interviewer and
      some of the interviewees also refer to a location known as 'Savage Creek'.
      We acknowledge the racism represented by this language and the harm it may
      cause our users. Providing access to these historical materials does not
      endorse any attitudes or behavior depicted therein." SCARC also points
      readers to its Anti-Racist Actions Statement.
    </p>

    <h2 id="context">Where the language comes from</h2>

    <p>
      The narrators were born between 1890 and 1930 and were speaking in the
      1970s through the 2000s about a valley and a coast range they had worked
      all their lives. The language appears in the narrators' answers, in the
      interviewer's questions, and in historical documents reprinted as
      appendices. The Library does not weigh those against each other, and does
      not tell a reader in advance whose words a passage carries: the page-level
      notes say what is on the page and where, and the page itself shows who
      said it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="disagree">If you think a note is wrong</h2>

    <p>
      Notes can be too heavy, too light, or in the wrong place. All three are
      correctable, and the Library would rather hear it than not:
      <a href="/about/corrections/">send a correction</a>. Descendants of
      narrators and of people named in the interviews are especially welcome to
      write; the Library will act on a family's request about personal
      information about their own relatives.
    </p>

    <p class="see-also">
      <a href="/collections/zybach/">Finding aid</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/provenance/">Provenance</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/colophon/">Colophon</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/rights/">Rights, use, and takedown</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/errata/">Errata</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</RoomLayout>
